Slide 9

Strategic Architecture Constraints & Release Considerations



Dimension	Challenge / Constraint	Implication for Release 1
Fabric Alignment	Capability must be exposed via Fabric as a managed access layer, not as an isolated application	Requires API-first design and integration model before scale-out
Group AI Integration Model	Strategic direction centralises model governance, guardrails and monitoring	Formalise prompt registry, audit logging, model lifecycle controls
Platform Deployment Alignment	Foundational architecture positions GCP/CIB authorised data platform as runtime target	Migration from PoC runtime to strategic environment required
Unstructured Data Governance	UDL strategy requires classification, retention and access controls at ingestion stage	Embed governance controls early in ingestion pipeline
Enterprise Storage Standardisation	Canonical artefacts and embeddings must align to platform storage standards	Define structured artefact schema & storage contracts
Agentic AI Control & Observability	Multi-step LLM orchestration introduces non-deterministic behaviour	Implement evaluation framework, monitoring and fallback strategies
Knowledge Drift & Ontology Evolution	As policies and procedures evolve, graph and lineage must remain consistent	Requires ontology governance model and version-aware KG builds
Scalability of Hybrid Retrieval	Graph + vector + LLM orchestration increases computational complexity	Validate performance characteristics under enterprise-scale load
Trust & Explainability Expectations	Regulatory environment requires explainable outputs	Evidence-first response model must be enforced by design
Operating Model Clarity	Strategic model spans CIB platform, Group AI and domain ownership	Define clear responsibility boundaries before productionisation



Short Verbal Framing:

“These are the strategic architecture constraints and industry realities that must be addressed as we evolve toward a scalable, governed Release 1 capability.”


Slide 10:

Release 1 formalises the PoC capabilities into a controlled production deployment within the approved CIB data platform.

It introduces enterprise-aligned storage, managed graph infrastructure, and governed LLM integration via Group AI, while maintaining scoped document onboarding and evidence-backed retrieval.

This interim design prioritises governance, operational stability and Fabric alignment, with broader automation and scale optimisation planned for future phases.
